        "markdown": "Chapter 1: Start.\n0:011 secondAll right, you guys. I would say happy Wednesday, but England just lost to Argentina, so my house is in a state of mourning. And unfortunately, I have more\n0:099 secondsbad news. Despite our best efforts, no one in the entire world reached out to our tip line yesterday in response to\n0:1818 secondsour silver alert. No one saw a bald man wandering around looking like he had a bad case of amnesia, marking time in\n0:2525 secondsSalt Lake City on September 10th. I am so sorry, Blakey Pooh. We are doing our best to help you fill in those blanks.\n0:3232 secondsWhat were you doing that morning in Salt Lake City? It's anybody's guess. And it turns out that Blake isn't the only person that is drawing blanks regarding\n0:4040 secondswhat transpired on September 10th. So today we are going to return to the courtroom where there was a day for testimony that was given um that put me\n0:4949 secondsimmediately on alert. A testimony that joged my memory regarding a a witness that I had spoken to. Also, apparently\n0:5656 secondsthere is such a thing as a widow who doesn't readily accept the fed narrative. Cory Comparatori, the lone\n1:041 minute, 4 secondsvictim of the Butler shooting, well, his wife has just come out and she has stated explicitly that she believes the government is engaged in a cover up of\n1:131 minute, 13 secondssorts, or at least not telling the truth about what happened on that fateful night in Butler, Pennsylvania. Imagine that. Welcome back to Candace.\nChapter 2: Quick updates.\n1:371 minute, 37 secondsOkay, so a couple of quick updates at the top here. We received a lot of emails from you guys. People that were sending us the inside of their freedom\n1:451 minute, 45 secondsshirts that they purchased uh they thought from Turning Point USA following Charlie's assassination. Well, foremost, you should know that those shirts were\n1:531 minute, 53 secondsnot actually sold by the charity. They made an insane profit by running those through uh the for-profit arm. But beyond that, they show they sold you a\n2:012 minutes, 1 secondcheaper version of what Charlie was wearing. So, you guys sent it were like, \"Can it's all cotton, the freedom shirt.\n2:062 minutes, 6 secondsIt's all cotton.\" Well, that's not what Charlie was wearing on September 10th.\n2:102 minutes, 10 secondsFor whatever reason, Charlie was wearing a customized car heart shirt, which was we are thinking it's got to be either\n2:172 minutes, 17 secondsthe one that is 50% polyester or 95% polyester. I'm going to show you that a photo of Charlie here and you can see in\n2:242 minutes, 24 secondsthe right hand corner we have that Carheart logo. So you guys were show sold a cheaper a cheaper version. It\n2:342 minutes, 34 secondswould however be interesting to know who had this custom shirt made for Charlie because I can tell you that that also is\n2:412 minutes, 41 secondsnew. That brand is new. I can at every Turning Point college stop that I ever did with Charlie, we just wore typically\n2:502 minutes, 50 secondsthe same t-shirts that the student chapter was wearing and they were provided for us when we arrived. We'd have like funny uh like you saw like a\n2:592 minutes, 59 secondshim to whatever was happening to be a little extra controversial. I have to say I don't think I have ever worn a polyester shirt at Turning Point um on\n3:083 minutes, 8 secondscampus ever. you could go back and look and let me know campus class, whatever the t-shirts were that reflected the tour that we were on is what we were\n3:153 minutes, 15 secondswearing. And here's something that's even more interesting. Remarkably, Megan Kelly, man, she's kind of the ground zero of she interviewed all these people\n3:233 minutes, 23 secondsearly on and now what they told her is becoming relevant. And she specifically happened to ask one of Charlie's many new assistants about the freedom shirt.\n3:343 minutes, 34 secondsAnd I'm sorry to report to you guys, but there is going to be just a little touch of amnesia in this clip as well from Charlie's, you know, second or third or\n3:433 minutes, 43 secondsfourth assistant, Nate Walker. Take a listen.\n3:473 minutes, 47 secondsYeah, I was Charlie's um executive assistant. Um the day-to-day Charlie, make sure he has his food, water, clothes. Um all the stuff that he\n3:563 minutes, 56 secondsdoesn't need to think about, I uh would do for him. And uh clothes, how'd you do clothes?\n4:014 minutes, 1 secondYeah, you figure that out. You uh it was difficult. Not not the easiest job.\n4:054 minutes, 5 secondsWe have the clothing racks in the warehouse.\n4:074 minutes, 7 secondsSome days I would just have to guess, figure out what is Charlie gonna want to wear today. And uh sometimes I'd be right, sometimes not. But well, can I ask you this is um not not\n4:154 minutes, 15 secondsto take it to a darker place, but we're all wearing these freedom shirts, the same shirt that Charlie was wearing. Yeah. On the day he was killed.\n4:234 minutes, 23 secondsWhat is there a story behind this shirt?\n4:254 minutes, 25 secondsYeah. So, we uh we usually bring a couple shirts um to the to the campus stops and uh I asked Charlie in the car.\n4:314 minutes, 31 secondsI go, \"Charlie, what what shirt do you want to wear?\" He goes, \"I want to wear the freedom shirt.\" That uh that's not controversial. What were the other options? I can't I can't remember.\n4:394 minutes, 39 secondsLike does he did he did he wear like the one that my son has with Trump giving double middle fingers? No. No. No. Twice. Not those ones.\n4:474 minutes, 47 secondsIt would always say something. Never surrender. Here I am. Freedom. It was he he liked his short tagline.\n4:584 minutes, 58 secondsLot happening there. Um okay. So, he went on and did this difficult task of picking out shirts. And then Mikey McCoy comes out and tries to make it seem like\n5:065 minutes, 6 secondsit was a really hard thing to pick out a t-shirt. We have the room. It's got a lot of t-shirts. And this guy Nate Walker like grabbed a couple and then I\n5:145 minutes, 14 secondsguess turned to Charlie and said, \"Which one would you like to wear?\" And Charlie said, \"I don't want to be controversial.\n5:185 minutes, 18 secondsI'm not known for being controversial.\" That's literally why he went on to college campuses and found founded Turning Point USA to bring the\n5:265 minutes, 26 secondscontroversy and the discussion there. I this is a Charlie that I really don't know. Maybe they're telling the truth.\n5:315 minutes, 31 secondsMaybe I really didn't know Charlie and he was just pretending for all these years because I the Charlie I knew we were dropping Blexit Black exit from the Democrat party. We were wearing him too\n5:395 minutes, 39 secondsduring the peak of Me Too. But Charlie this time said I'd like a Carheart shirt because it says freedom and because it's\n5:485 minutes, 48 secondsnot controversial. And then when she asked what were the other options, he's like I don't remember. Wow. He just blacked out on what other t-shirts he\n5:565 minutes, 56 secondspicked. How many options did Charlie need? When did Charlie turn into such a diva? I have so many questions. I just remember us just putting on whatever t-shirt and not even caring at all about\n6:046 minutes, 4 secondsthat. Anyways, there's that. So, I did happen to ask some uh Turning Point employees, former and current, if Cart was one of their vendors, and I was told\n6:136 minutes, 13 secondsno cuz it's quite an expensive shirt actually. And I don't remember us having ever worked with them. And so, there you\n6:226 minutes, 22 secondsgo. If you happen to know anything about how or if there was just this one custom shirt made for Charlie, please do send\n6:316 minutes, 31 secondsus an email at um more tips at candacewowens.com.\n6:366 minutes, 36 secondsIt's just interesting. Another quick update and a thank you also to the person who sent us over the tip that Frank Churik also worked with Bank of\n6:456 minutes, 45 secondsAmerica. We did that little chart for you yesterday and we're like, \"Wow, all of these people's parents were just working at Bank of America Barrel LED at the exact same time. How interesting.\n6:556 minutes, 55 secondsHow fascinating.\" And it turns out Frank Churik it gets to join the crew for 15 years. He worked as a consultant in New Jersey to Bank of America until 2011.\n7:067 minutes, 6 secondsWho knew? I I I actually am wondering if he happened to cross Tyler Boyer's dad who temporarily located Tyler and his family to New Jersey during that exact\n7:147 minutes, 14 secondssame time frame because he needed to serve as an executive to the Bank of America. It is just incredible, you know, from Bank of America to politics.\n7:227 minutes, 22 secondsIt kind of makes me want to open a Meil Lynch account, right? Check out this headline following Frank Churik's ouster. tells us uh this is the\n7:297 minutes, 29 secondsChristian Post reporting back in 2011 that he was the Christian consultant and was fired over his book on gay marriage.\n7:387 minutes, 38 secondsThe article tells us that just months after being fired from Cisco Systems in California over an anti-gay marriage book, Christian consultant Dr. Frank\n7:467 minutes, 46 secondsTurk was also given the boot from Bank of America. Turk was doing work on and off for Bank of America for about 15 years, mainly conducting leadership and\n7:547 minutes, 54 secondsteam building programs. Other clients have included Coca-Cola, Home Depot, among others. The US Navy veteran was hired in May to present at a meeting of\n8:048 minutes, 4 secondsBank of America's global business management analysis team, which is within the global wealth and investment\n8:128 minutes, 12 secondsmanagement. Wealth and investment. We talked about that yesterday.\n8:168 minutes, 16 secondsI'm just wondering if did you guys maybe not maybe maybe Bank of America is just a really big bank and you guys all\n8:248 minutes, 24 secondshappen to be attached to it at the same time during the same years. The more you know. Anyway, fascinating little tidbit.\nChapter 3: Interesting statement at the hearing and first hand account of the shot.\n8:308 minutes, 30 secondsAnyway, today what I wanted to discuss with you guys is another portion of the Tyler Robinson hearing. A portion which quite literally made me sit up straight.\n8:388 minutes, 38 secondsIt was a testimony that was given by Sergeant Jennifer Filomina. She is within the UVU Department of Public\n8:468 minutes, 46 secondsSafety and I was sitting on a couch taking notes on everything with um Carrie Priene Bowler on my side. We were\n8:548 minutes, 54 secondson a couch in Spain and she will tell you I completely freaked out. I actually said wait rewind that rewind that I'm having a memory. It's floating from the\n9:019 minutes, 1 secondback to the front of my mind. I was a little bit panicked about it. I made her uh just over and over again and then I was texting Skyler. Whoa, whoa, whoa. It\n9:109 minutes, 10 secondswas a particular portion that caused me to react that way. I want you to listen uh to what Sergeant Filamina says. And more importantly, I want you to pay\n9:189 minutes, 18 secondsattention to her demeanor, the social cues. She's clearly uncomfortable and she's going to suffer from a little bit of amnesia.\n9:259 minutes, 25 secondsIt's going around. Everyone is suddenly finding Dory. Take a listen.\n9:319 minutes, 31 secondsYeah, of course. Um when officers were canvasing the scene, um they were also trying to see right uh the route of the shooter as well as just trying to find\n9:399 minutes, 39 secondsthe shooter. and they deem or they determined that the Fulton Library stood out because there were um unattended\n9:479 minutes, 47 secondsitems there at the bus stops that were away from the courtyard area and so to them they thought it was suspicious.\n9:579 minutes, 57 secondsAnd were certain items seized from and what were they?\n10:0010 minutesYes, you had a backpack um a jacket as well as some gloves and there may have been some other things. I just don't recall them right now. And do you recall\n10:0910 minutes, 9 secondswhat the item numbers were of those seizures? Was it nine? Does that ring a bell? I don't remember.\n10:1410 minutes, 14 secondsUh those whatever was seized was sent to the FBI lab for processing. Yes.\n10:2110 minutes, 21 secondsUm and along with some information that the suspect may have uh shed some of this these items as he fled. Correct.\n10:2810 minutes, 28 secondsYes.\n10:2910 minutes, 29 secondsWas a decision at some time made and and you were requesting that both DNA and other type of analysis get done?\n10:3610 minutes, 36 secondsYes. At some point, did you decide to pull a plug on that?\n10:4010 minutes, 40 secondsUm, at the time when it was already sent, we didn't have more information as far as whether or not whe whether or not\n10:4710 minutes, 47 secondsthe evidence that were collected there were probative, but eventually you communicated to the lab that those items were not in fact\n10:5610 minutes, 56 secondsconnected with the case and there was no need to do further testing. I don't remember communicating that.\n11:0111 minutes, 1 secondOkay. If that's in the notes of the FBI or the ATF, would that be inconsistent with what you know?\n11:0811 minutes, 8 secondsUh, no.\n11:1011 minutes, 10 secondsOkay. I'm mentioning because some of the reports, the lab reports have those items listed. Okay.\n11:1611 minutes, 16 secondsI just want to be clear that those items eventually you determined were not related to this case. Would that be fair?\n11:2311 minutes, 23 secondsYeah, that would be fair.\n11:2611 minutes, 26 secondsIt's a weird time for your memory to go out. who found in let's call this like the biggest manhunt plausibly ever, biggest assassination without question\n11:3411 minutes, 34 secondssince JFK. I think bigger than the JFK assassination. Uh global interest in this case. You're on the manhunt. You find unattended items.\n11:4411 minutes, 44 secondsUh a backpack, a jacket and gloves.\n11:4811 minutes, 48 secondsThat's interesting because it was like what 90 degrees. So, what kind of gloves are we talking that you found unattended\n11:5511 minutes, 55 secondsat the bus stop by the library? And I wish we had showed you the leadup to this question because uh what he's essentially asking her is, you know, you\n12:0312 minutes, 3 secondshave made it clear that the person who jumped off Losi Center ran this particular route. Just so you guys know, the library is not near the Loy Center.\n12:1012 minutes, 10 secondsSo, the reason why they that route became significant and why they noticed these abandoned odd items of which she's only recalling in her fuzzy memory three\n12:1912 minutes, 19 secondsof them. But he goes on to say, \"There was actually nine and you guys wanted it to be tested and then suddenly told the feds not to test it.\" Do you remember\n12:2712 minutes, 27 secondstelling the feds not to test it? I don't remember.\n12:3112 minutes, 31 secondsOkay. Well, it's it's in it's in their notes. Is it correct? Yeah, it's in the notes. It's correct. You don't remember telling them not to test?\n12:4012 minutes, 40 secondsI a Who was wearing a jacket and gloves? Hm.\n12:4812 minutes, 48 secondsAt near the Fulton Library. We're going to talk about why that is relevant.\n12:5112 minutes, 51 secondsAnyway, uh the reason that those details immediately jog my memory was because of that route. Right. Back in the early\n12:5812 minutes, 58 secondsinvestigation, my early investigation, someone had told me specifically that they were there um by the bus stop, and they actually ended up making a report\n13:0713 minutes, 7 secondsabout having seen the alleged shooter, someone on the roof, and had a totally different description of what the person\n13:1613 minutes, 16 secondslooked like. uh describe the person as wearing all black tactical gear and intentionally having every piece of\n13:2313 minutes, 23 secondstheir body covered in black tactical gear. Tactical gloves you might think. I don't think we're talking winter gloves.\n13:3013 minutes, 30 secondsLike I said about and somebody can tell me in the chat here. I I think it was literally like 85 degrees in Utah. So I\n13:3813 minutes, 38 secondsI think if you found gloves, it's just relevant in general. Who's wearing, bringing, holding, and dropping gloves?\n13:4413 minutes, 44 secondsAnd what were the other six items that were found? Well, this individual um told me that he spoke to students at the\n13:5213 minutes, 52 secondsbus stop in front of a library and he also described the gun and he was able to do that because he happened to also\n13:5913 minutes, 59 secondsbe the only person who captured the gunman on Loi Center in a video as he\n14:0614 minutes, 6 secondsbegan lying prone for the shot. You guys remember this video? It was the most widely spread video on that day, I believe. Um, beyond the video of Charlie\n14:1514 minutes, 15 secondsactually getting hit, the I'm talking about the w the most widely spread video of the shooter allegedly on the rooftop.\n14:2214 minutes, 22 secondsThis is he's the first one to have circulated that. And I'm going to let you watch the video so you know specifically which one I'm talking about.\n14:3014 minutes, 30 secondsRight up.\n14:3114 minutes, 31 secondsYeah. You think he'd already be in place? You would think that.\n14:3614 minutes, 36 secondsSo, there's somebody on the roof right there. Just saying. He just ran from uh over Where's my finger? Well, go from\n14:4514 minutes, 45 secondsover there. Ran in and now he's right there. Just saying.\n14:5214 minutes, 52 secondsThe person who recorded this footage is the person that I tracked down. It was not easy to track him down, but I've stayed in touch with him since, and he has never changed his description of the\n14:5914 minutes, 59 secondsperson that he saw on the rooftop and the person that he saw run away off of the rooftop thereafter. He described an individual, like I said, in all black\n15:0715 minutes, 7 secondsand in tactical gear, possibly be wearing a helmet. And after he realized that the police had arrested the wrong\n15:1415 minutes, 14 secondsperson, I'm referring to decoy boy George Zen, he walked over to the UVU command center and filed a report and said, \"That's not the guy. That can't be\n15:2315 minutes, 23 secondsthe guy. He's just he's too old.\" The feds only contacted him once a few days later. He has never been called by the\n15:3115 minutes, 31 secondsprosecution. It's weird. You got this case coming up. You would think he would reach out to this person who recorded the video and had an eyewitness account.\n15:3915 minutes, 39 secondsUm, he's also willing to testify. He would be a very credible witness because it happens to be that he works with\n15:4615 minutes, 46 secondsguns. I want you to take a listen to what he told me. And for reference, uh, this is discussion was had on September\n15:5515 minutes, 55 seconds25th. So, this was just 15 days following Charlie's assassination. And uh I have intentionally, just so you\n16:0216 minutes, 2 secondsknow, edited out any identifying information um regarding who this person is for now until he wants to fully come forward. Take a listen.\n16:1116 minutes, 11 secondsI um about 5 seconds after I stopped recording is when that person fired.\n16:1716 minutes, 17 secondsUm and I saw and heard everything but a muzzle flash. So I didn't see a flash come from\n16:2516 minutes, 25 secondsthe edge of his gun. As you can see in the video, I couldn't see the gun at the time. It was just the head and shoulders on a roof.\n16:3116 minutes, 31 secondsBut we felt like the you know the little concussive impact from the shot going off. It sounded I thought it sounded like a a\n16:3916 minutes, 39 secondssharp um muzzle report. It sounded more like an AR than a large bore hunting rifle, but of course I've never heard a\n16:4816 minutes, 48 secondsgun fire, you know, around buildings like that. I'm always out outside in the desert or something something like that.\n16:5416 minutes, 54 secondsSo yes, in your video you say that you witnessed him running and the feds reports say that he dropped onto that Loy Center roof at 12:15.\n17:0417 minutes, 4 secondsSo did where did you see him running?\n17:0617 minutes, 6 secondsWas it right that he like instantly run and dive into the shot or did he did you see him run and then started recording after he ran?\n17:1717 minutes, 17 secondsYeah. So he it was definitely not 12:15.\n17:2017 minutes, 20 secondsIt was it was uh 22 if anything. He was he was in our line of view for about 30\n17:2717 minutes, 27 secondsseconds total and he ran into off I mean we saw him clear the edge of the\n17:3517 minutes, 35 secondsbuilding and Bob up and down. He would he would go down and look around and then run stand up and run maybe 6 ft\n17:4317 minutes, 43 secondsforward and then go down look around again up six feet forward until he got to the edge of the roof. So, it looked like a sort of a tactical movement, I would say, which is why I started\n17:5117 minutes, 51 secondsrecording because it was I assumed to be a security guard for the event.\n17:5517 minutes, 55 secondsUm, okay. And that's why I started recording is because the security guard would be heading towards the main tent and be moving up and down with with a\n18:0318 minutes, 3 secondslong gun in his hand. I was like, that's a little strange. Let's at least record this because because this guy just\n18:1018 minutes, 10 secondsSo, by the time I had my phone out, he was in position and I was of course only able to film the head and shoulders with him viewing the event there on the roof.\n18:1918 minutes, 19 secondsAnd yo